NNAAMMEE
       pcnfsd, rpc.pcnfsd - (PC)NFS authentication and print request server

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       //uussrr//ssbbiinn//rrppcc..ppccnnffssdd

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ppccnnffssdd is an RPC server that supports Sun ONC clients on PC (DOS, OS/2,
       Macintosh,  and  other) systems. This page describes version two of the
       ppccnnffssdd server.

       rrppcc..ppccnnffssdd may be started from //eettcc//rrcc..llooccaall or by the iinneettdd(8)  super‐
       daemon.  It  reads  the configuration file //eettcc//ppccnnffssdd..ccoonnff if present,
       and then services RPC requests directed to program number 150001.  This
       release  of  the ppccnnffssdd daemon supports both version 1 and version 2 of
       the pcnfsd protocol. Consult the rrppccggeenn source file  ppccnnffssdd..xx  for  de‐
       tails of the protocols.

       The requests serviced by ppccnnffssdd fall into three categories: authentica‐
       tion,  printing,  and  other. Only the authentication and printing ser‐
       vices have administrative significance.

AAUUTTHHEENNTTIICCAATTIIOONN
       When ppccnnffssdd receives a PPCCNNFFSSDD__AAUUTTHH or  PPCCNNFFSSDD22__AAUUTTHH  request,  it  will
       "log in" the user by validating the username and password and returning
       the  corresponding  uid, gids, home directory, and umask. If ppccnnffssdd was
       built with the WWTTMMPP compile‐time option, it will also append  a  record
       to  the  wwttmmpp(5) data base. If you do not wish to record PC "logins" in
       this way, you should add a line of the form

              wwttmmpp ooffff

       to the //eettcc//ppccnnffssdd..ccoonnff file.

PPRRIINNTTIINNGG
       ppccnnffssdd supports a printing model based on the use of  NFS  to  transfer
       the  actual print data from the client to the server. The client system
       issues a PPCCNNFFSSDD__PPRR__IINNIITT or PPCCNNFFSSDD22__PPRR__IINNIITT request, and the server  re‐
       turns  the path to a spool directory which the client may use and which
       is exported by NFS.  ppccnnffssdd creates a  subdirectory  for  each  of  its
       clients:  the parent directory is normally //eexxppoorrtt//ppccnnffss and the subdi‐
       rectory is the hostname of the client system.  If you  wish  to  use  a
       different parent directory, you should add a line of the form

              ssppoooollddiirr _p_a_t_h

       to the //eettcc//ppccnnffssdd..ccoonnff file.

       Once  a client has mounted the spool directory using NFS and has trans‐
       ferred print data to a file in this directory, it  will  issue  a  PPCCNN‐‐
       FFSSDD__PPRR__SSTTAARRTT  or  PPCCNNFFSSDD22__PPRR__SSTTAARRTT  request.   ppccnnffssdd handles this, and
       most other print‐related requests, by constructing a command  based  on
       the  printing services of the server operating system and executing the
       command using the identity of the PC user.  Since  this  involves  set‐
       user‐id privileges, ppccnnffssdd must be run as root.

       Every  print  request  from the client includes the name of the printer
       which is to be used. In SunOS, this name corresponds to a printer defi‐
       nition in the //eettcc//pprriinnttccaapp(5) database. If you wish to define  a  non‐
       standard  way of processing print data, you should define a new printer
       and arrange for the client to print to this printer. There are two ways
       of setting up a new printer.  The first involves the addition of an en‐
       try to //eettcc//pprriinnttccaapp(5) and the creation of filters to perform the  re‐
       quired processing. This is outside the scope of this discussion. In ad‐
       dition,  ppccnnffssdd  includes  a mechanism by which  you can define virtual
       printers known only to ppccnnffssdd clients. Each printer  is  defined  by  a
       line in the //eettcc//ppccnnffssdd..ccoonnff file of the following form

              pprriinntteerr _n_a_m_e _a_l_i_a_s_‐_f_o_r _c_o_m_m_a_n_d

       _n_a_m_e  is  the  name of the printer you want to define. _a_l_i_a_s_‐_f_o_r is the
       name of a "real" printer which corresponds to this printer.  For  exam‐
       ple,  a  request  to display the queue for _n_a_m_e will be translated into
       the corresponding request for the printer _a_l_i_a_s_‐_f_o_r. If  you  have  de‐
       fined  a printer in such a way that there is no "real" printer to which
       it corresponds, use a single "‐" for this field. (See the definition of
       the printer tteesstt below for an example.) _c_o_m_m_a_n_d is a command which will
       be executed whenever a file is printed on _n_a_m_e.  This command  is  exe‐
       cuted by the Bourne shell, //bbiinn//sshh using the ‐‐cc option. For complex op‐
       erations  you  should  construct an executable shell program and invoke
       that in _c_o_m_m_a_n_d.  Within _c_o_m_m_a_n_d the following tokens will be replaced:

       TTookkeenn     SSuubbssttiittuuttiioonn

       $$FFIILLEE     Replaced by the full path name of the print data  file.  When
                 the command has been executed, the file will be unlinked.

       $$UUSSEERR     Replaced  by the username of the user logged in to the client
                 system.

       $$HHOOSSTT     Replaced by the host name of the client system.

       Consider the following example //eettcc//ppccnnffssdd..ccoonnff file:

              printer rotated lw /usr/local/bin/enscript ‐2r $FILE
              printer test ‐ /usr/bin/cp $FILE /usr/tmp/$HOST‐$USER

       If a client system prints a job on the printer rroottaatteedd the utility  eenn‐‐
       ssccrriipptt will be invoked to pre‐process the file $FILE. In this case, the
       ‐‐22rr  option  causes the file to be printed in two‐column rotated format
       on the default PostScript® printer.  If the client requests a  list  of
       the  print  queue for the printer rroottaatteedd the ppccnnffssdd daemon will trans‐
       late this into a request for a listing for the printer llww..

       The printer tteesstt is used only  for  testing.  Any  file  sent  to  this
       printer  will  be copied into //uussrr//ttmmpp..  Any request to list the queue,
       check the status, etc. of printer tteesstt will  be  rejected  because  the
       _a_l_i_a_s_‐_f_o_r has been specified as "‐".

